Error naming is inconsistent

The project exports RsError, while alerting expects RamShieldError. Pick one public name. The least disruptive fix is:

`rust
pub type RamShieldError = RsError;
`

But consistently using RsError is cleaner.

IPC correctness and security
Never turn invalid IP input into 0.0.0.0

This pattern appears repeatedly:

`rust
ip.parse().unwrapor(IpAddr::V4(Ipv4Addr::UNSPECIFIED))
`

That means "garbage", "", and "999.999.999.999" all operate on the same key. Return a structured error instead:

`rust
fn parseip(value: &str) -> Result<std::net::IpAddr, Response> {
    value.parse().maperr(|| Response::Error {
        code: 400,
        message: format!("invalid IP address: {value}"),
    })
}
`

Then:

`rust
Request::CheckIp { ip } => {
    let parsed = match parseip(&ip) {
        Ok(ip) => ip,
        Err(resp) => return resp,
    };

    let status = store.get(&crate::storage::ipkey(parsed));
    match status {
        Some(crate::storage::Value::IpRecord(rec)) => {
            let reason = match &rec.blockstate {
                crate::storage::BlockState::Blocked { reason, .. } => {
                    Some(reason.tostring())
                }
                 => None,
            };

            Response::IpStatus {
                ip,
                blocked: matches!(
                    rec.blockstate,
                    crate::storage::BlockState::Blocked { .. }
                ),
                threat: rec.threatscore,
                ewmarps: rec.ewmarps,
                reason,
            }
        }
         => Response::IpStatus {
            ip,
            blocked: false,
            threat: 0.0,
            ewmarps: 0.0,
            reason: None,
        },
    }
}
`

The current code also treats Suspicious as blocked in CheckIp, while DetectionEngine::isblocked() treats only Blocked as blocked. That semantic mismatch should be removed.

Administrative commands are no-ops

These branches lie to the caller:

`rust
Request::BlockIp { ... } => Response::Ok {
    message: format!("blocked ..."),
},
Request::UnblockIp { ... } => Response::Ok {
    message: format!("unblocked ..."),
},
Request::Flush => Response::Ok { message: "flushed".into() },
`

Implement real state changes. A basic manual block method could be:

`rust
impl Store {
    pub fn setblockstate(
        &self,
        ip: IpAddr,
        state: BlockState,
        ttlsecs: Option<u64>,
        ramlimitbytes: usize,
    ) -> Result<()> {
        let key = ipkey(ip);
        let mut rec = match self.get(&key) {
            Some(Value::IpRecord(rec)) => rec,
             => IpRecord::new(ip, 32),
        };

        rec.blockstate = state;
        self.insert(key, Value::IpRecord(rec), ttlsecs, ramlimitbytes)
    }
}
`

Then pass the engine/config into request processing and actually mutate the store. Flush needs a command channel to the detection worker; returning success without triggering a flush must be removed.

Blocking channel send inside async code

The single report path uses:

`rust
eventtx.send(ev)
`

crossbeamchannel::Sender::send blocks when the channel is full. Because this happens in an async connection task, it can block the runtime thread.

Use trysend consistently:

`rust
match eventtx.trysend(ev) {
    Ok(()) => Response::Ok {
        message: "accepted".into(),
    },
    Err() => {
        droppedevents.fetchadd(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
        Response::BatchOk {
            accepted: 0,
            rejected: 1,
        }
    }
}
`

Also increment the shared metric, not only the server-local counter:

`rust
engine.metrics.increjected(1);
`

At present, dashboard eventsrejected remains zero while the IPC server tracks dropped events separately.

Batch size handling silently discards excess input

ReportConnections deserializes an arbitrarily large vector, processes only 4096 events, then silently ignores the remainder. Reject oversized batches before iteration:

`rust
if events.len() > BATCHMAX {
    return Response::Error {
        code: 413,
        message: format!("batch exceeds maximum of {BATCHMAX} events"),
    };
}
`

A deserialization limit is also necessary because the whole JSON value is allocated before this check. The existing 1 MB connection limit partly constrains it, but it applies cumulatively per connection, not cleanly per request.

Response write errors are ignored

This only checks whether the timeout elapsed:

`rust
if timeout(config.writetimeout, writeresp(...)).await.iserr()
`

An inner I/O error is treated as success. Match both levels:

`rust
match timeout(config.writetimeout, writeresp(&mut socket, &resp)).await {
    Ok(Ok(())) => {}
    Ok(Err(e)) => return Err(e),
    Err() => {
        debug!("Write timeout");
        return Ok(());
    }
}
`

Idle timeout does not behave as intended

The read timeout is 5 seconds and returns immediately on timeout, so the 30-second idle timeout is effectively unreachable. Either:

• Treat a read timeout as a loop continuation until total idle time is exceeded, or
• Remove the separate idle timeout.

Example:

`rust
Err() => {
    if lastactivity.elapsed() >= config.idletimeout {
        return Ok(());
    }
    continue;
}
`

Add authentication and safer binding defaults

The dashboard allows config writes, and the IPC allows blocking operations, without authentication. CorsLayer::permissive() makes browser-origin attacks easier if bound beyond loopback.

Minimum fixes:

• Bind control interfaces to loopback by default.
• Require a bearer token or mutually authenticated TLS.
• Remove permissive CORS unless a separate frontend requires it.
• Split read-only monitoring routes from administrative routes.
• Apply request body limits.
• Reject startup when an admin endpoint is bound publicly without authentication.

Detection pipeline correctness
Pre-aggregation destroys event data

processeventintopreaggs() does not initialize:

• firsttsns
• protofp

It also accepts only status codes 100-599, while IpAgg::absorb() maps all values into a bucket with saturating arithmetic.

Then flushpreaggstostore() reconstructs one event per original event with:

`rust
statuscode: 0,
protofingerprint: 0,
timestampns: agg.lasttsns,
`

This loses the status distribution and first timestamp, performs a potentially huge allocation, and defeats pre-aggregation.

Do not reconstruct events. Refactor the batch processor to consume aggregates directly:

`rust
fn processeventintopreaggs(&self, ev: ConnectionEvent) {
    let mut entry = self.preaggs.entry(ev.ip).ordefault();
    entry.absorb(&ev);
}

fn flushpreaggstostore(&self) {
    self.lastpreaggsflushns
        .store(nowns(), Ordering::Relaxed);

    if self.preaggs.isempty() {
        return;
    }

    let aggs: HashMap<IpAddr, IpAgg> = self
        .preaggs
        .iter()
        .map(|entry| (entry.key(), entry.value().clone()))
        .collect();

    self.preaggs.clear();

    let totalevents = aggs.values().map(|a| u64::from(a.count)).sum();
    self.metrics.incingested(totalevents);

    let mut subnetcounts = HashMap::<u32, u32>::new();
    for (ip, agg) in &aggs {
        if let Some(sk) = subnetkey(ip) {
            subnetcounts.entry(sk).ordefault() += agg.count;
        }
    }

    self.flushaggregates(aggs, subnetcounts, totalevents);
}
`

Move the body of flushbatch() into flushaggregates(). This removes the expensive expansion from aggregates back into events.

Event and byte counters can overflow

IpAgg::absorb() uses plain addition:

`rust
self.count += 1;
self.bytes += ev.bytes;
`

Use saturating operations for attacker-controlled volumes:

`rust
self.count = self.count.saturatingadd(1);
self.bytes = self.bytes.saturatingadd(ev.bytes);
self.statusdist[bucket] = self.statusdist[bucket].saturatingadd(1);
`

Bloom filter can panic with zero bits

BloomFilter::new(0) creates zero words and later performs modulo zero. Validation currently rejects only values below 100,000, but direct construction remains unsafe.

`rust
pub fn new(bitcount: usize) -> Self {
    let words = bitcount.max(64).divceil(64);
    Self {
        bits: vec![0; words],
        size: words  64,
    }
}
`

Poisoned RwLock panics the detection thread

These calls panic after lock poisoning:

`rust
self.bloom.read().unwrap()
self.bloom.write().unwrap()
`

Prefer parkinglot::RwLock, or recover explicitly:

`rust
let bloomhit = self
    .bloom
    .read()
    .unwraporelse(|e| e.intoinner())
    .contains(ip);
`

Worker count is ignored

spawnworkers(self, n: usize) always starts exactly one batch processor. Either rename/remove the parameter or implement the configured worker model. Given the single shared pre-aggregation map, one aggregator thread is reasonable, but the configuration should not claim otherwise.

Thread spawn uses expect

Production comments emphasize no panics, but:

`rust
.spawn(...)
.expect("spawn batch processor");
`

Return a Result from spawnworkers and propagate it:

`rust
pub fn spawnworkers(self: Arc<Self>) -> std::io::Result<()> {
    let eng = self.clone();
    std::thread::Builder::new()
        .name("rs-batch".into())
        .spawn(move || eng.batchprocessorloop())?;
    // ...
    Ok(())
}
`

Subnet blocking still performs a full-store scan

A reverse index was implemented in Store, but it is never populated in mergerecord() and never used by subnetbatchloop().

After storing an IP record:

`rust
self.store.updatesubnetindex(
    &key,
    subnetkey(ip),
    false,
);
`

Then replace:

`rust
for e in self.store.inner().iter() {
`

with:

`rust
for key in self.store.getipsinsubnet(sk) {
    let Some(Value::IpRecord(rec)) = self.store.get(&key) else {
        continue;
    };
    // block rec.ip
}
`

Update the index on removal and expiry as well, or it will accumulate stale keys.

Subnet counters are accumulated, not windowed

mergesubnetwindow() adds to totalrps forever until a subnet is blocked and reset. The field is not RPS and not reliably a window. A low, steady rate eventually reaches the threshold.

Store a window start or use per-interval replacement:

`rust
pub struct SubnetRecord {
    // ...
    pub windowevents: u64,
    pub windowstartedns: u64,
}
`

Reset based on ratewindowsecs, not only after blocking.

Rate calculation uses record lifetime, then halves count

The instantaneous rate is:

`rust
requestcount / (now - firstseen)
`

Afterward, if the window expired, count is halved and firstseen is reset. This mixes lifetime averaging with an approximate window and produces unstable results.

Calculate batch/window rate from aggregate timestamps:

`rust
let elapsedns = agg.lasttsns.saturatingsub(agg.firsttsns).max(1);
let samplerps = agg.count as f64  1000000000.0 / elapsedns as f64;
rec.ewmarps = ewma(rec.ewmarps, samplerps);
`

For events sharing one timestamp, use the configured batch window as the minimum denominator.

First timestamps can be zero

Because pre-aggregation fails to initialize firsttsns, a new record may start at epoch zero, making elapsed time enormous and RPS nearly zero. Fixing aggregate preservation resolves this.

Shutdown and lifecycle
Engine shutdown is not shared with detection

Engine has:

`rust
shutdown: AtomicBool
`

But detection receives a new independent value:

`rust
Arc::new(AtomicBool::new(false))
`

Therefore engine.shutdown() never stops the detection worker.

Change the engine field to shared state:

`rust
pub struct Engine {
    // ...
    shutdown: Arc<AtomicBool>,
}

impl Engine {
    pub fn new(...) -> Self {
        Self {
            // ...
            shutdown: Arc::new(AtomicBool::new(false)),
        }
    }
}
`

Then pass:

`rust
engine.shutdown.clone()
`

Forecasting has no shutdown path

Forecaster::run() loops forever. Add a cancellation signal, preferably tokioutil::sync::CancellationToken:

`rust
pub async fn run(self: Arc<Self>, shutdown: CancellationToken) {
    let mut t1 = tokio::time::interval(Duration::fromsecs(1));
    let mut t5 = tokio::time::interval(Duration::fromsecs(5));

    loop {
        tokio::select! {
             = shutdown.cancelled() => break,
             = t1.tick() => self.tickhw(&mut hw, &mut history).await,
             = t5.tick() => self.tickentropy().await,
        }
    }
}
`

The dashboard, uptime task, TTL wheel, alerting task, and IPC server should use the same cancellation mechanism.

Main shutdown loop always waits until timeout

After calling:

`rust
engine.shutdown();
`

this condition remains true forever:

`rust
while engine.isshuttingdown() && now < deadline
`

isshuttingdown() reports a requested state, not completion. Track task handles and await completion instead.

A better engine API is:

`rust
pub struct EngineHandle {
    joins: Vec<tokio::task::JoinHandle<()>>,
    shutdown: CancellationToken,
}

impl EngineHandle {
    pub async fn shutdown(self) {
        self.shutdown.cancel();
        for join in self.joins {
            let  = join.await;
        }
    }
}
`

Cache evictor is detached and sleeps for five seconds

Dropping Cache signals shutdown but does not join the thread. The captured Arc<DashMap> keeps storage alive until the sleeper wakes. Store the JoinHandle and use a condition variable or channel for prompt shutdown.

Also, the documented statement "Cache<K,V> is Send + Sync" is fine, but "all operations are lock-free at the DashMap shard level" is inaccurate. DashMap uses sharded locking.

Store and memory accounting
Replacement RAM accounting is wrong

Current logic:

`rust
let netgrowth = entrysize.saturatingsub(oldsize);
self.rambytes.fetchadd(netgrowth, Ordering::Relaxed);
`

If an entry shrinks, the counter is not reduced. Worse, capacity enforcement only rolls back net-new entries:

`rust
if oldsize == 0 && current + netgrowth > ramlimitbytes
`

A larger replacement can exceed the RAM limit.

Use signed adjustment and restore the old entry on failure:

`rust
pub fn insert(
    &self,
    key: String,
    value: Value,
    ttlsecs: Option<u64>,
    ramlimitbytes: usize,
) -> Result<()> {
    let expiresat = ttlsecs.map(|s| Instant::now() + Duration::fromsecs(s));
    let newentry = Entry {
        value,
        expiresat,
        version: 0,
    };

    let keybytes = key.len();
    let newsize =
        keybytes + std::mem::sizeof::<Entry>() + newentry.value.heapbytes();

    let old = self.inner.insert(key.clone(), newentry);
    let oldsize = old.asref().mapor(0, |entry| {
        keybytes + std::mem::sizeof::<Entry>() + entry.value.heapbytes()
    });

    let current = self.rambytes.load(Ordering::Acquire);
    let projected = current
        .saturatingsub(oldsize)
        .saturatingadd(newsize);

    if projected > ramlimitbytes {
        match old {
            Some(oldentry) => {
                self.inner.insert(key, oldentry);
            }
            None => {
                self.inner.remove(&key);
            }
        }
        return Err(RsError::CapacityExceeded {
            limitmb: ramlimitbytes / (1024  1024),
        });
    }

    self.rambytes.store(projected, Ordering::Release);
    self.totalinserts.fetchadd(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
    Ok(())
}
`

For strict correctness under concurrent inserts, this requires reservation via fetchupdate, a mutex around budget accounting, or a permit-based allocator. The load/store version can lose updates.

increment() bypasses all accounting and limits

increment() creates entries without increasing rambytes, without honoring TTL or capacity, and without incrementing totalinserts.

Route all creation through a consistent accounting path, or document increment() as internal and include its storage in the budget.

Lazy expiration does not remove entries

Store::get() returns None for expired entries but leaves them in the map and RAM count:

`rust
if entry.isexpired() {
    return None;
}
`

Remove atomically:

`rust
pub fn get(&self, key: &str) -> Option<Value> {
    if let Some(entry) = self.inner.get(key) {
        if !entry.isexpired() {
            return Some(entry.value.clone());
        }
    }

    if let Some((k, entry)) =
        self.inner.removeif(key, |, entry| entry.isexpired())
    {
        let freed =
            k.len() + std::mem::sizeof::<Entry>() + entry.value.heapbytes();
        self.rambytes.fetchsub(freed, Ordering::Relaxed);
        self.totalevictions.fetchadd(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
    }

    None
}
`

Cache count can underflow during concurrent expiry

In Cache::get(), after dropping the map reference:

`rust
self.inner.remove(key);
self.count.fetchsub(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
`

Another thread may already have removed the key. Decrement only when removal succeeds:

`rust
if self.inner.remove(key).issome() {
    self.count.fetchsub(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
}
`

The background evictor and clear() can also race, making the approximate counter wrap to a huge u64. If only approximate size is required, use inner.len(); otherwise update count through atomic removal paths with stronger invariants.

Cache capacity is unused

capacity is only exposed by an accessor. Inserts can grow without bound. Either rename it capacityhint, or enforce it.

A simple policy:

`rust
if old.isnone() && self.inner.len() > self.capacity {
    // Evict expired entries first, then reject or evict according to policy.
}
`

For a real cache, choose and document an eviction strategy such as LRU, TinyLFU, or random sampling.

Nanosecond epoch conversion truncates

`rust
d.asnanos() as u64
`

This wraps after the value exceeds u64. Current epoch nanoseconds fit, but a safe conversion is:

`rust
u64::tryfrom(d.asnanos()).unwrapor(u64::MAX)
`

A monotonic Instant is preferable for TTL calculations because wall-clock adjustments can cause premature or delayed expiration.

Metrics and observability
Detection blocks are double-accounted across paths

flushbatch() calls recordblock() for each block and later recordbatch() increments blocksdetection by the batch block count. That is acceptable only because recordblock() currently updates the log but not counters. Meanwhile blockstotal and incblocks() are mostly unused.

Define one canonical counter update point:

`rust
pub fn recordblock(&self, ip: &str, reason: &str, module: BlockModule) {
    self.blockstotal.fetchadd(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
    match module {
        BlockModule::Detection => {
            self.blocksdetection.fetchadd(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
        }
        BlockModule::Subnet => {
            self.blockssubnet.fetchadd(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
        }
        BlockModule::Forecast => {
            self.blocksforecast.fetchadd(1, Ordering::Relaxed);
        }
    }
    // append log
}
`

Then remove block counter increments from recordbatch() and forecasting/subnet call sites.

Blocks are logged before they are applied

The broadcast sends decisions, but no block consumer is shown. Detection logs and increments block metrics before any state change. Consequently:

• blocksapplied rises.
• StoreStats.blocked stays zero.
• CheckIp reports unblocked.
• Repeated decisions can be emitted.

Add a block-applier task that consumes BlockDecision, updates IpRecord.blockstate, schedules TTL, appends WAL, and only then records an applied block.

A clean flow is:

`mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[Detection or forecasting] --> B[BlockDecision channel]
    B --> C[Block applier]
    C --> D[Store state]
    C --> E[TTL wheel]
    C --> F[WAL]
    C --> G[Applied-block metrics]
`

Dashboard module statistics are placeholders

Engine::getmodulestats() returns zeros despite Metrics::getmodulestatsdata() already existing. Replace it:

`rust
pub fn getmodulestats(&self) -> Vec<ModuleStats> {
    let stats = self.store.getstats();
    self.metrics.getmodulestatsdata(
        stats.uptimesecs,
        self.metrics.eventsingested.load(Ordering::Relaxed),
        0,
        stats.ipstracked,
        stats.rambytes,
        stats.ramlimitmb,
    )
}
`

Channel depth should be wired from detection rather than hardcoded to zero.

System memory metric is mislabeled

getsystemusage() returns total system memory:

`rust
let totalmemorymb = sys.totalmemory()
`

But the dashboard field is named memoryusagemb. Use process memory if supported by the selected sysinfo version, or rename it to systemmemorytotalmb.

Health is always healthy

dashboardsnapshot() hardcodes:

`rust
ishealthy: true,
healthreason: "running".into(),
`

Health should consider:

• Engine shutdown state.
• IPC bind/start success.
• Queue saturation.
• Detection worker liveness.
• Forecast task liveness.
• RAM pressure.
• WAL write failures.
• Recent processing progress.

Forecast input is not RPS

eventslastsecond receives the latest flush's event count. Flushes are configured around 1 second currently, but can occur due to size or timing. Convert using measured interval:

`rust
rps = eventcount as f64 / elapsedseconds
`

Store both the count and the measurement duration, or maintain a rolling per-second counter.

Alert entropy condition appears inverted

Alerting warns when:

`rust
entropy >= entropyalertthreshold
`

Forecasting treats low entropy as suspicious:

`rust
if h < minentropy
`

Unless these are intentionally different alerts, alerting should likely check entropy <= threshold.

