Live config does not reach the actual pipeline

bootpipeline() creates a new independent handle:

`rust
let cfghandle = cfgsnapshot.clone().intohandle();
`

Dashboard updates engine.config, but detection reads the separate cfghandle. Therefore dashboard configuration changes appear successful but do not affect detection.

Pass engine.config.clone() directly:

`rust
let detection = Arc::new(DetectionEngine::new(
    store.clone(),
    engine.config.clone(),
    // ...
));
`

Forecasting currently owns a static ForecastingConfig; make it read the shared config handle if live updates are advertised.

IPC defaults behave inconsistently

IpcConfig::default() sets optional limits to None, despite serde defaults producing Some(...) when fields are absent during deserialization. Thus:

• Config::default() yields None.
• Deserializing an empty [ipc] can yield Some(default).

The server compensates with unwrapor, but serialized config differs depending on construction path. Set explicit defaults consistently:

`rust
maxconnectionbytes: defaultmaxconnectionbytes(),
readtimeoutms: defaultreadtimeoutms(),
writetimeoutms: defaultwritetimeoutms(),
connectionidletimeoutms: defaultconnectionidletimeoutms(),
`

Better yet, make these non-optional values.

Environment parsing silently ignores invalid values

Silently retaining defaults can hide deployment mistakes. Return an error containing the variable name:

`rust
fn envparse<T>(name: &str) -> anyhow::Result<Option<T>>
where
    T: std::str::FromStr,
    T::Err: std::fmt::Display,
{
    match std::env::var(name) {
        Ok(value) => value
            .parse()
            .map(Some)
            .maperr(|e| anyhow::anyhow!("{name}={value:?}: {e}")),
        Err(std::env::VarError::NotPresent) => Ok(None),
        Err(e) => Err(e.into()),
    }
}
`

Validate all denominator and wheel fields

Missing validation includes:

• ratewindowsecs > 0
• ttlwheelresolutionms > 0
• ttlwheelsize > 0
• preaggsflushintervalms > 0
• hwbeta and hwgamma in [0,1]
• minentropy >= 0
• IPC byte and timeout values greater than zero
• WAL segment size greater than header size

TtlWheel::new(0, 0) currently enables division or modulo by zero.

Dashboard has DOM XSS exposure

The frontend inserts API-controlled strings into innerHTML, including IP, reason, module, and labels:

`javascript
tb.innerHTML = blocks.map(b => ...${b.reason}...).join('');
`

Those values may originate from user-provided manual block reasons. Use DOM APIs and textContent, or escape HTML before interpolation.

At minimum:

`javascript
const escapeHtml = value => String(value)
  .replaceAll('&', '&amp;')
  .replaceAll('<', '&lt;')
  .replaceAll('>', '&gt;')
  .replaceAll('"', '&quot;')
  .replaceAll("'", '&#039;');
`

Then escape every interpolated string. DOM construction with textContent is safer.

WAL, audit logs, and persistence
WAL always opens segment zero

On restart, Wal::open() appends to wal-00000000.rshw and initializes seg to zero even if later segments exist. It should discover the highest valid segment and append there.

Also, segment rotation after writing means a record can exceed the configured maximum. Rotate before append when the current non-empty segment plus record size would exceed the limit.

Segment sorting should be numeric

Lexicographic sorting happens to work with eight-digit names until the index exceeds the width. Parse segment indices and sort numerically.

WAL replay silently tolerates partial trailing headers

The loop stops when fewer than HEADER bytes remain and reports success. This may be intentional crash recovery, but it conflicts with the corruption test expectation for arbitrary short garbage. Define policy explicitly:

• Ignore only a trailing partial record in the final segment.
• Reject malformed data in non-final segments.
• Return replay warnings/repair metadata.

Audit logging blocks async-adjacent execution

AlertingEngine::writeauditlog() performs synchronous open and write for every alert. Although maybealert() itself is synchronous, it is called from an async task and can block the runtime.

Use a dedicated writer task/channel, keep the file open, emit JSON Lines, and implement the promised rotation. The current module documentation says "audit log rotation", but no rotation exists.

For compliance claims, also add:

• Tamper evidence or signed hashes.
• Restricted file permissions.
• Retention policy.
• Structured event IDs.
• Actor and outcome fields.
• Clock/source metadata.

The comment "ISO 27001 compliant" should be removed; an enum cannot itself establish compliance.

Blob store trusts handle lengths

BlobStore::read() allocates compressedlen bytes directly. If handles can be loaded from untrusted persistence, enforce maximum compressed and original sizes and verify originallen against decompressed output.

Forecasting, DNS, and learning
Holt-Winters anomaly logic compares actual against updated forecast

update(y) consumes the actual value and returns a forecast after incorporating it. The anomaly score then compares that same actual value to the post-update forecast, dampening anomalies.

Forecast first, score residual, then update the model:

`rust
pub fn forecast(&self) -> f64 {
    let seasonal = self.seasonal[self.tick % self.period];
    (self.level + self.trend + seasonal).max(0.0)
}
`

In the tick:

`rust
let forecast = hw.forecast();
let z = hw.zscore(rps, forecast, hist.std().max(1.0));
hw.update(rps);
hist.push(rps);
`

History should preferably contain residuals, not raw RPS, for the z-score denominator.

Disabled forecasting is ignored

ForecastingConfig.enabled is validated and configurable, but bootpipeline() always starts the forecaster. Guard startup or let the task observe live configuration.

Entropy data represents only the latest flush

subnetwindow is replaced on each flush. If flush cadence varies, entropy samples are incomparable. Aggregate into a fixed time window.

DNS pattern matching is overly broad

This logic:

`rust
domain.contains("com")
`

classifies any string containing "com" as generic, including unrelated labels. Parse domain labels and public suffixes rather than substring matching.

Likewise, "bot" in a legitimate domain automatically yields at least 0.9 threat. This is prone to false positives. Normalize case, handle internationalized names, and base scoring on labels and observed behavior.

DNS recency score is always added

lastseen is set to SystemTime::now() immediately before calculating the score, so the recent-activity +0.1 always applies. If that is intended, it is not a useful feature. Score inactivity relative to a previous timestamp before updating it.

Pattern similarity is not meaningful sequence similarity

This code:

`rust
a.iter().filter(|&x| b.contains(x)).count()
`

counts repeated bytes in a whenever each byte exists anywhere in b. It ignores order and frequency in b, and can produce misleadingly high similarity.

For byte-pattern detection, consider:

• Normalized edit distance.
• N-gram Jaccard similarity.
• Cosine similarity over byte-frequency vectors.
• SimHash/MinHash for approximate matching.

Also cap sourceips, bytepatterns, and entropyhistory; currently they can grow indefinitely.

Threat averaging is biased

For a new pattern:

`rust
threatlevel = (0.0 + threatlevel) / 2.0
`

The first observation is halved. Subsequent updates are an exponential average with fixed 0.5 weighting, not a true average.

Use:

`rust
let previouscount = pattern.frequency;
pattern.frequency += 1;
pattern.threatlevel =
    (pattern.threatlevel * previouscount as f32 + threatlevel)
    / pattern.frequency as f32;
`

Stub modules should be clearly marked or removed

These functions return constant zero:

`rust
engine::learning::score()
learning::xgboost::score()
`

preprocess::normalize() returns input unchanged, and PredictionEngine::trainmodel() sets accuracy to 0.95 without training.

Do not expose these as working ML features. Either implement them, feature-gate them as experimental, or return an explicit unsupported error.

Smaller correctness and quality issues
• BoundedVecDeque::new(0) is broken. push() sees length equal to capacity, pops nothing, then pushes and grows. Reject zero or make pushes no-op.
• Duplicate cache documentation appears above get().
• src/c.txt contains stray content and should be removed.
• README is stale. It says storage is only foundational and Bloom integration is pending, while both exist. It also advertises port 7891 although dashboard defaults to 9999.
• README claims zero allocation, but aggregation creates multiple maps/vectors and pre-aggregation reconstructs events.
• main silently ignores unknown CLI arguments. Use clap for the daemon too.
• canonicalize(...).tostr().unwrap() can panic on non-UTF-8 paths. Accept Path/PathBuf in config loading.
• Panic hook is never installed. Call ramshield::installpanichook() at startup if desired.
• Engine::start() is a misleading no-op. Remove it or rename it to reflect that it only logs.
• startasync() uses a current-thread runtime, while spawning long-lived tasks and a server. This can work, but one accidental blocking operation stalls the whole pipeline.
• DashboardSnapshot.channeldepth and pipeline values are hardcoded. Either populate them or omit them.
• Hot subnets are unsorted and unbounded. Sort descending and cap the result.
• Audit alert history uses Vec::remove(0), which is O(n). Use VecDeque.
• Alert cooldown multiplication can overflow. Use saturatingmul(1000).
• Status code bucketing is inconsistent between IpAgg::absorb() and pre-aggregation.
• TTL zero semantics conflict. Cache uses zero as "never expire"; store uses zero as "expire immediately". Standardize or document clearly.
• BlockState::Suspicious is treated inconsistently across code paths.
• CORS should not be permissive on config mutation routes.
• Google Fonts adds an external runtime dependency and may violate restricted-network deployments or privacy expectations.

Recommended implementation order
Phase 1: Make behavior truthful and safe
Fix missing alerting types or remove the module.
Reject invalid IP addresses.
Implement actual block/unblock/flush operations.
Add the block-applier consumer and update store state before metrics.
Share one shutdown token across all workers.
Validate dashboard config patches.
Remove permissive CORS and add authentication.
Replace blocking channel sends with trysend.

Phase 2: Restore detection correctness
Process IpAgg directly without event reconstruction.
Preserve first timestamp, status distribution, bytes, and fingerprint.
Replace lifetime-average RPS with fixed-window samples.
Make subnet counters truly windowed.
Populate and use the subnet reverse index.
Fix forecasting input units and pre-update residual scoring.

Phase 3: Fix memory and persistence
Correct replacement RAM accounting.
Remove expired entries on access.
Bring increment() under accounting.
Enforce or remove cache capacity.
Join background threads during shutdown.
Discover the latest WAL segment on startup.
Add bounded allocation checks to WAL/blob replay.

Phase 4: Align observability and product claims
Centralize block metrics.
Use real module statistics.
Implement meaningful health checks.
Mark ML and DNS heuristics as experimental.
Update README architecture, ports, and performance claims.

Essential regression tests

Add tests for these exact cases:

• Invalid IP requests return an error and never touch 0.0.0.0.
• Manual block changes CheckIp, GetIpStats`, and store statistics.
• Unblock reverses that state.
• Detection shutdown terminates its OS thread.
• Forecaster stops on cancellation.
• Pre-aggregation preserves first/last timestamps and status buckets.
• Store replacement with a smaller value reduces RAM usage.
• Store replacement with a larger value respects the RAM limit and restores the old value on failure.
• Concurrent lazy expiration never underflows counters.
• Runtime config patch rejects invalid values.
• Runtime config changes are observed by detection.
• Oversized IPC batches are rejected rather than truncated.
• Failed writes terminate a connection cleanly.
• Subnet block lookup uses the reverse index.
• WAL restart continues from the latest segment.
• Dashboard renders hostile block reasons as text, not HTML.

The most important architectural change is to make block decisions flow through one authoritative block-applier, and to make all pipeline components share one configuration handle and one shutdown token. Those changes resolve many of the current inconsistencies at once.
